module gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired

import std.types { Bool, Int, String, List }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size }
import std.optional { Present, Absent }
import std.list { first_where }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }
import product.host_identity { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts { remote_build_host_is_serving }
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity { operator_host_srv14 }
import gunbc.design_ruling { srv14_serving_memory_charge_retirement_operator_approved_2026_11_24 }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance { dashboard_instance_for_host, DashboardInstanceForHost, DashboardInstanceHostUnknown }

// THE PER-SLOT ROW, APART FROM THE FLEET ALLOCATION THAT CONSUMES IT. What one runner slot is
// granted is a leaf fact; gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation derives widths, walls and floor-outcome
// receipts over it. While the row lived there, every reader of the ceiling -- the dashboard's
// dispatch memory ceiling, and through it the approval broker -- compiled the floor-outcome
// vocabulary (gunbc.ci.ci_failure_class), merge admission and the v2 compiler behind them.

// THE SLOT'S CPU CEILING IS A POLICY OF THE ROW, AND THE ROW'S POLICY IS NO CEILING. CpuQuotaResolved
// is an absolute CPUQuota= of that many threads; SlotCpuQuotaUnbounded is the affirmative declaration
// that the slot carries no absolute ceiling and is governed by cpu_weight alone. There is no refusing
// arm: the value used to be derived from a vendor survey that could fail to resolve, and that survey
// is deleted (gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot_policy), so nothing upstream can refuse.
type RunnerSlotCpuQuota
  = CpuQuotaResolved { threads: HardwareThreadCount }
  | SlotCpuQuotaUnbounded

type RunnerSlotLabels {
  github_labels: List<String>
}

type RunnerSlotDesired {
  memory_max: ByteSize
  memory_swap_max: ByteSize
  memory_high: ByteSize
  tasks_max: Int
  cpu_weight: Int
  cpu_quota: RunnerSlotCpuQuota
  labels: RunnerSlotLabels
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note: String = "WHY 16GiB/15GiB, SUPERSEDING THE 14GiB/13GiB ROW (operator ruling 2026-08-17). The 2026-08-05 derivation below is not repudiated — it is re-run on a changed input. Its subject is floor-job cgroup peaks, and the floor's peak moved: the claim-scope cut measures 15.6GB (14.53GiB) after taking whole-run peak from 45.4GB to 15.6GB. 14.53GiB does not fit a 14GiB MemoryMax and sits 1.53GiB above a 13GiB MemoryHigh, so the prior row can no longer hold its own consumer.\n\nTHE PIN IS OBSERVED, NOT PREDICTED (srv1, read 2026-08-17, gunbc_srv1_converged_slot_throttle_observation). All five srv1 slots carry the declared 14GiB/13GiB. Two of them — srv1-04 and srv1-05 — report memory.peak of EXACTLY 13.00GiB against 829 and 44803 memory.high events, with memory.max events and oom_kill both zero. A peak equal to the throttle line is the censored lower bound this very note taught for the four 15.0GiB pins in the 2026-08-05 sample; it is the workload being held, not the workload being measured. Survival without OOM is reclaim plus swap, and the host carried 10GiB of swap in use at the same reading.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS, STATED BECAUSE IT IS NOT FREE: two runner slots fleet-wide. At 16GiB the managed hosts admit 6 rather than 7 (7 x 16 = 112GiB against 111.4GiB available) while srv1/srv2 hold 5 (5 x 16 = 80GiB against 81.0GiB, a 1.0GiB margin — the tightest number in gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation and the first thing to recheck if the sessions reservation or the legacy overhead is ever corrected upward). Fleet total 24 -> 22. The trade is bought deliberately: a 15GiB/14GiB row would have preserved all 24 slots and cleared MemoryMax, but its throttle line would sit 0.53GiB BELOW the measured peak, making the throttle confound permanent and structural in the instrument now measuring the fold.\n\nTHAT THE NEW TARGET EQUALS srv2-03'S DRIFTED READBACK IS A COINCIDENCE AND MUST NOT BE READ AS A PRECEDENT. gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation still stands unamended: this row moved because a floor-peak measurement moved, which is the same subject the 2026-08-05 ruling used, and it would have moved to 16GiB had every host in the fleet been sitting at 8. srv1 was CONVERGED to 14/13 when this was written, so the fleet was not at 16/15 to be reconciled toward.\n\nDISSOLVE-ON: the 14.53GiB peak is not established as steady. If the fold's remaining 5.6GB comes down, re-derive from the new floor-peak distribution and reclaim the two slots; this row is sized to a measurement under active reduction, not to a settled envelope.\n\nSUPERSEDED DERIVATION, RETAINED AS THE PRIOR INPUT (operator ruling 2026-08-05). 186 floor-job cgroup peaks from 2026-08-05 plus five sampled days back to 2026-07-16, read from the [calibration] floor_peak_post receipts the ci job emits: successful runs peak at p50 10.32GiB and max 10.96GiB, and NOT ONE run in the sample reached the 16GiB MemoryMax. Four of 186 reached 15.0GiB, which is the 15GiB MemoryHigh exactly — those are throttle pins, censored lower bounds rather than demand, and they are why the cap does not fall further.\n\nTHE FLOOR UNDER IT IS ALREADY MEASURED AND IT IS 12GiB: gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget, enforced by claim_executor as an arm-time refusal. MemoryHigh is what the run-time governor arms against, so MemoryHigh is the axis that number constrains — 13GiB leaves exactly 1GiB of margin over it, and clears the worst p90 in the three-week sample (12.94GiB on 2026-07-30). Going to 13/12 to buy an eighth slot on the managed hosts would put the throttle line ON the measured refusal floor with zero margin, and the 2026-07-30 distribution says that day would have spent most of its runs in swap.\n\nWHAT MOVING 16 TO 14 IS NOT: it is not oversubscription and does not touch the guaranteed-mode property. Every slot still owns its ceiling outright. The ceilings are simply sized closer to what the workload was measured to want, which is what freed the width."

// 🟡 REGISTERED FOLLOW-UP — task-budget envelope model (operator ruling on PR 8045, 2026-08-09).
// tasks_max=16384 below is APPROVED as an EMERGENCY PARITY ROLLBACK ONLY: it is not accepted as
// a derived value, and RunnerSlotDesired.tasks_max is not accepted as the model home. The
// follow-up model must distinguish THREE task budgets, each derived from one measured envelope
// (concurrent-build admission × compiler/thread fan-out): (1) runner-slot task budget; (2) shared
// compile-pool task budget — sccache cache-miss compiles run as children of the SERVER'S cgroup,
// not the requester's (gunbc.build_cache.build_cache_instance placement axis), so the pool owns their task
// count exactly as compile_pool owns their memory; (3) host-parent reserve — with the conservation
// law sum(slots) + pool + reserve <= parent ancestor limit. Dissolve-on: that model landing, at
// which point this row's tasks_max derives rather than being authored. Do not build it in PR 8045.
// ── THE 2026-09-12 FLOOR-DEMAND RULING: 26 GiB MemoryMax / 25 GiB MemoryHigh ────────────────
//
// THE PRIOR 16/15 ROW IS NOT REPUDIATED; IT IS RE-RUN ON A MEASURED INPUT, exactly as the
// 2026-08-17 ruling re-ran the 14/13 row on its own. Its subject is the floor job's cgroup
// demand, and that demand is now measured DIRECTLY rather than inferred from throttle pins.
//
// WHY THE OLD ROW COULD NOT SEE THIS. Every reading behind 16/15 was a PIN: peaks landing on the
// MemoryHigh line, which that ruling itself correctly named "the censored lower bound ... the
// workload being held, not the workload being measured". A censored sample cannot report how far
// past the line the workload wanted to go, so the row could only ever be raised to just above the
// last pin, and would pin again.
//
// THE UNCENSORED MEASUREMENT, taken on the lane CI actually runs rather than on all lanes in one
// process:
//   command  claim_executor --required-ci --required-lane witnesses
//                           --source-root dag --source-root src/v2
//   commit   975706293d0f02c83ecda5f978607ad9e54b11b8
//   binary   sha256 5cc271009d241d4049df1e5db5162fc3ca8af70c7d391dee3ee993cec2b7a471
//   host     srv1, systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=64G, MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2
//   budgets  max=68719476736  high=max   <- NOT constrained; this is the check that makes the
//                                           reading a demand rather than another pin
//   verdict  FloorClean  planned=3762 executed=3762 claims_failed=0  phases_run=3 phases_failed=0
//   peak     whole-job scope 24412725248 = 22.74 GiB
//            the run heartbeat's own cgroup-charge reading peaked at 24.7 GiB
//   stall    0 faults/min -- no thrashing, corroborating that nothing held it
//
// THE TWO READINGS DISAGREE AND THE LARGER ONE IS TAKEN. A charge reading above the scope peak
// should not happen if both name the same cgroup, so one of them names a different level and this
// row does not know which. Sizing to the smaller figure to save a slot, while unable to explain
// the larger, is the fail-open direction; the grain rule this module already applies -- round up,
// in the fail-closed direction -- lands 24.7 GiB on 25 GiB. MemoryHigh is the axis the run-time
// governor arms against, so 25 GiB is the throttle line and MemoryMax is the authored max-1 GiB
// relation above it at 26 GiB.
//
// IT IS AN OBSERVED DEMAND FOR ONE EXECUTION, not an established ceiling. One run cannot bound a
// distribution, and this row does not claim it does; what it claims is that a line BELOW a
// measured uncensored peak is known to be too low, which needs no distribution to be true.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, AND BOTH AXES MOVE BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a second edit. The memory axis
// is now the envelope quotient, so srv1 goes 25 -> 15 and srv3/srv4 29 -> 17 on this literal alone.
// THE CPU AXIS NO LONGER MOVES WITH THIS LITERAL (2026-09-19). Until that date
// `gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot` derived cores from THIS ceiling against a vendor memory-per-vCPU
// ratio, so 16 -> 26 GiB took cores_per_slot 6 -> 10 and the CPU axis 128/6 = 21 down to
// 128/10 = 12, binding the fleet at 12/12/12 -- eight of the twenty-two lost slots bought by CPU
// quota nothing asked for, since the floor run uses about one core sustained (cpu 20 min against
// 24 min wall). That coupling was accepted on 2026-09-12 and REPAIRED on 2026-09-19 by the ruling
// recorded at gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot_policy: cores_per_slot is 1, the CPU axis admits 128 and
// does not bind, and the memory admissions 15 and 17 ARE the committed widths.
//
// The whole trade is bought deliberately: at 16/15 the floor welds to the throttle line on every
// run and its verdict is decided by whether the working set happens to drift back under, which is
// not a CI system.
//
// ── srv2 IS NOT SAFE AT THIS ROW AND THE HELD ARM HIDES IT ────────────────────────────────────
//
// THIS IS A DECLARED HAZARD IN THIS DIFF, NOT A RESOLVED ONE. The per-slot row is FLEET-UNIFORM, so
// raising it raises srv2 too -- and srv2's five HELD slots at 26 GiB demand 130 GiB against a
// measured 125.09 GiB of usable RAM (`gunbc.host.host_memory_observation` `gunbc_ci_srv2_memtotal_measured`,
// 134314512384 bytes), BEFORE any overhead or session charge. At 16 GiB the same five demanded
// 80 GiB and fit with room; the hazard is created by this row, not inherited.
//
// WHY NO WALL CAUGHT IT, which is the part worth keeping. srv2's width comes from the HELD arm,
// which exists precisely because its session denominator is unestablished -- so there is no
// envelope to divide and the conservation checks have no denominator to test against. The held
// value is a DESCRIPTION of what the host currently runs, and a description does not stop being
// reported when the declaration moves underneath it. `runner_slot_allocation_wall_holds` and
// `runner_slot_allocation_oversubscription_boundary_exact` both pass, correctly, because neither
// can reach a host whose envelope is unestablished. The deleted held arm's note recorded that a
// held width must not outrank a zero-memory refusal; this is the same class one step out. Both
// arms are now gone: `gunbc_runner_committed_width` checks the slot shape before the per-host
// derivation and answers RunnerWidthUnresolved for an unestablished envelope.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THE HEADLINE NUMBER: 41 is NOT a safe uniform target. It counts srv2 at five
// slots it cannot hold at this size. srv2 needs its own derivation -- or exclusion from the raise,
// which the fleet-uniform row cannot currently express -- before any total including it is quoted.
//
// AND 41 IS NOT THE CI-SERVING NUMBER EITHER. `host_github_slot_count` is the committed width MINUS
// `host_fabric_slot_count`, so some committed slots are fabric execution members rather than GitHub
// runners. Every total in this note is an ALLOCATION count; the serving count is lower and is the
// one a capacity conversation actually wants. SINCE 2026-11-09 THE GAP IS SRV1'S ALONE: srv3 and srv4
// returned their two each to GitHub Actions (gunbc.design_ruling::srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci), so
// on those hosts the serving count equals the allocation and the only host still short of its own
// allocation is srv1, whose shakedown and revision-job slots stay carved. A total quoted across all
// four hosts is still an allocation count and still over-states what serves CI.
//
// dissolve-on: the floor's demand falls below this line. The prepared-product and dependency
// lifetime reductions have a measured exchange rate now -- roughly one slot per GiB removed on
// srv1 -- so this row is sized to a workload under active reduction and is expected to fall.
// THE BYTE COUNT IS THE AUTHORITY AND THE ByteSize IS DERIVED FROM IT, so the number has one home
// and the width arithmetic below stays in the Int domain. Mixing Int and Nat in one expression is a
// corpus-wide hazard rather than a local inconvenience -- one such expression in std.nat broke
// resolution in unrelated modules -- so the quotient is taken over byte COUNTS and the measure is
// reconstructed for the consumers that want a ByteSize.
// ── THE 2026-10-09 RULING: 22 GiB MemoryMax / 21 GiB MemoryHigh / NO SWAP (the v1 withdrawal) -- SIZES SUPERSEDED 2026-11-04 BELOW, kept as the record ──
//
// The floor this row was sized for is gone: the required job is the native emit-and-build alone
// (gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow), whose measured peak on the former lane was about 17.7-18.0 GB
// with no swap across 281 jobs (operator's receipt, side-chat review of gunbc#13658). The operator
// ruled the envelope: a FIXED hard maximum of 22 GiB, swap refused outright ("no thrashing allowed
// ever"), and a lower trip for the compiler itself -- gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate
// native_step_memory_trip, DERIVED from this row's MemoryHigh less that gate's declared headroom
// (20 GiB today), to be bound per subject step by gunbc test once a fleet job can own a bounded
// cgroup (gunbc_runner_slot_job_cgroup_ownership below; until that fact flips every subject step
// REQUIRES this slot's envelope and is receipted at its grain) -- so the kernel will trip the step
// under the throttle line and two GiB before it could reach this maximum. MemoryHigh stays the
// authored max-minus-one line. The 26/25 derivation below this note is superseded and kept as the record of
// why the number was what it was.
//
// WHAT "NO SWAP" BUYS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT (correction, 2026-11-03; gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
// swap_zero_read_as_a_thrash_refusal). The ruling's intent -- "no thrashing allowed ever" -- is a
// LIVENESS property, and a slot shape cannot discharge it. MemorySwapMax 0 forbids swap-backed
// relief: an overrun is never paged to disk beside its neighbours. It does NOT make the overrun
// end. Above memory.high the kernel throttles and reclaims and never kills for it; with swap
// refused only file-backed pages can go, so a working set over the line can refault its own text
// below memory.max indefinitely, with no OOM kill and no swap for the exit-time envelope verdict
// to read. This row therefore says only what is ADMITTED. Whether a run stays live under it is
// observed while it runs, from outside it, by gunbc.host.memory_stall_refusal
// supervised_subject_verdict; terminal evidence at exit stays gunbc.host.memory_envelope's.
//
// ── THE 2026-11-04 RULING: 25 GiB MemoryMax, NO THROTTLE LINE BELOW IT, NO SWAP (overrun must crash) ──
//
// Operator, 2026-11-04, to quick-newt-573: "i intended for that limit to crash those jobs, not slow
// them - thats a bug right? You can raise it to 25gib, but we need to figure out how to prevent this
// from not crashing in the future".
//
// ROOT CAUSE (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary), by the slot-control receipts below. MemoryHigh below MemoryMax is a
// THROTTLE line: the kernel slows and reclaims above it and never kills for it. The control
// (qual/slot-overrun-control, srv1 2026-11-04, scaled to a 2 GiB envelope, swap 0) read:
//   today's shape (high < max)   plain overrun: throttled 23 s (5083 high events) THEN killed;
//                                reclaim-thrash: TIMEOUT 75 s, 135044 high events, NO OOM  <- the bug
//   MemoryHigh == MemoryMax     (the representation below) plain overrun killed in 1 s, no throttle phase
//   MemoryHigh removed           plain overrun: killed in 1 s;
//                                reclaim-thrash: TIMEOUT 75 s, 0 high events, NO OOM
//   oomd pressure kill, 40%      plain overrun: kernel kill (oomd silent); reclaim-thrash: TIMEOUT
//                                150 s, oomd monitoring the unit AND a parent slice, PSI full avg10
//                                only ~7% -- the stall is slow progress, not stalled time
// So the throttle line is removed (memory_high IS memory_max, one byte count, so no band exists in
// which an overrun is slowed rather than killed), and an overrun that allocates is killed at the
// maximum. What no cgroup setting can do is end a run that never allocates past the maximum and
// never makes progress (anon + hot file set over the line, refaulting below memory.max, no OOM):
// that LIVENESS stall is the progress watchdog's (gunbc.host.memory_stall_refusal
// supervised_subject_verdict), keyed on PROGRESS and major faults per minute, never on
// memory.current -- measured good private-lane runs reach 24.8-25.2 GiB of slot memory.current
// INCLUDING reclaimable page cache, so a size-keyed kill would kill good runs. oomd is not the
// instrument (its key is PSI stall time, ~7% here).
//
// HEADROOM, recorded so growth is visible before it crashes: the kernel kills on UNRECLAIMABLE
// memory (cache is reclaimed first). Measured unreclaimable peaks: seal-path process 20.95 and
// 23.35 GiB (2 s sampling, n=2) against the 25 GiB maximum = about 1.65 GiB of headroom, thin.
data gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_bytes: Int = 26843545600

data gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max: ByteSize = byte_size(gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_bytes)

// THE BUILD-POOL CARVE, DECIDED FROM HOST FACTS, AND ONLY ON A HOST THAT IS ON THE ROSTER.
// A remote-build host with no dashboard instance has no serving reservation reaching the envelope
// through gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation host_memory_reserved (unlike srv1, whose serving
// reservation does), so its build pool needs a CI runner slot carved for it. The operator's standing
// ruling (the admitted-scaffold provenance on
// gunbc.design_ruling.remote_build_ssh_dispatch_is_an_admitted_scaffold) carves ONE CI runner slot on
// such a host for that pool. WHICH hosts are carved is read from host facts, not a hand-written host
// list (review 95809): the host must be (a) on the serving roster -- gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts
// remote_build_host_is_serving -- AND (b) resolve no dashboard instance, i.e. have no instance to carry
// its own reservation (dashboard_instance_for_host is HostUnknown). Exactly one slot,
// gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max, is given to the pool; every other host carves nothing (byte_size(0)).
// This is the SINGLE place that decides the carve; gunbc.compute.host_capacity host_capacity_build_seat
// READS THIS SAME function (divided across the pool's compute_pool_slots seats) as what it hands the
// pool, so the width reduction and the ledger appropriation are the ONE carve derived once from a single
// source -- never two numbers that could drift (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority).
//
// THE CARVE FOLLOWS ADMISSION, SO MEMORY IS NEVER SUBTRACTED WITHOUT BEING HELD (review 95809,
// gunbc.design_argument::consumption). Gating on the roster is what keeps the two sides of the carve
// together: a host is carved ONLY when it is on the roster, and it is admitted to the roster only when
// PR 5 has created and read back its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity home (the same "row and directory in
// ONE change" rule this PR applies to the roster). So in THIS PR, with srv2/srv3/srv4 off the roster, NO
// host is carved: srv3/srv4 keep their FULL committed runner width and nothing is subtracted, because
// nothing would hold the memory. When PR 5 admits srv3/srv4 (creating their home, adding the row), the
// carve and the ledger appropriation fire in that same change -- the slot leaves CI and is held by the
// pool atomically. (Today srv1 is the only rostered host; it resolves a dashboard instance and so is
// NOT carved -- its reservation reaches the envelope through host_memory_reserved, not a carve.)
//
// CONSERVATION AGAINST THE WALL (gunbc.ci.ci_runner_placement), stated rather than rewired. That wall is
// an UPPER BOUND: it sums each host's committed runner width priced at the slot, plus the per-host
// session slice and fixed overhead, and requires the total <= OS-usable RAM. A carve only ever makes the
// runner side SMALLER, and the carved slot is held once on the ledger side by the pool appropriation --
// and, because the carve is roster-gated, only ever for a host whose ledger pool actually exists. So the
// slot has genuinely MOVED from the CI side to the ledger side of the same machine; the runner-side sum
// never exceeds what it was before the host joined, and conservation stays gunbc.ci.ci_runner_placement's
// to certify. There is no revision at which a slot is removed from CI without being held by a pool -- the
// bug an ungated carve would leave between this PR and PR 5 (a full slot per host lost to neither side).
// THE CARVE AS A PREDICATE ON A HOST, so a reader that must branch on "is this host carved" asks the SAME
// question host_build_pool_reservation asks instead of re-deriving the two host facts or testing the
// reservation amount for > 0 (which conflates "not carved" with a real zero carve). The carve rule itself
// stays one function (build_pool_carve_is_due); this only applies it to a host.
fn host_build_pool_is_carved(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  build_pool_carve_is_due(rostered: remote_build_host_is_serving(host: host), carries_own_ledger: host_carries_own_ledger(host))
}

// THE POOL'S RESERVATION: CARVED FROM CI WHERE THE HOST PAYS FROM CI, RULED WHERE A RULING SAID IT HAS NO
// CI TO PAY FROM. Both forms fire ONLY for a rostered host (the carve follows admission, above); which form
// answers is the ruled-table question below. For every host without a ruling this is byte-for-byte the old
// carve: due -> exactly one CI runner slot, not due -> zero.
fn host_build_pool_reservation(host: HostIdentity) -> ByteSize {
  if remote_build_host_is_serving(host: host) {
    build_pool_reservation_of_ruling(
      ruled: host_ruled_build_pool_reservation(host: host),
      carve_due: host_build_pool_is_carved(host: host),
    )
  } else {
    byte_size(0)
  }
}

// THE CARVE RULE AS A PURE FUNCTION OF THE TWO HOST FACTS, so the POSITIVE case is witnessed even though
// PR 4 has no rostered host that lacks an instance (review 95809 / manager): a slot is carved for a build
// pool IFF the host is on the serving roster AND does not carry its own ledger. host_build_pool_reservation
// applies this to a real host; the positive witness cell exercises all three host-fact shapes so PR 5's
// admission flips a PROVEN rule (rostered + no instance -> exactly one slot) rather than an unexercised one.
fn build_pool_carve_is_due(rostered: Bool, carries_own_ledger: Bool) -> Bool {
  rostered && !carries_own_ledger
}

// A host "carries its own ledger" when it resolves a dashboard instance -- that instance's reservation
// reaches the CI envelope through gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation host_memory_reserved, so the host
// needs no carved build-pool slot. A rostered host that resolves NO instance (a build host like srv3/
// srv4 once PR 5 admits them) is the one whose pool must be carved from a CI slot.
fn host_carries_own_ledger(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  match dashboard_instance_for_host(host: host as String) {
    DashboardInstanceHostUnknown { host: _ } => false
    DashboardInstanceForHost { instance: _ } => true
  }
}

// THE RULED BUILD POOL -- THE BUILD POOL OF A HOST THAT HOLDS NO CI SLOTS BY RULING.
// The carve above is a TRANSFER: one CI runner slot leaves CI and is held by the pool. A transfer needs a
// transferor. gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation::ruled_zero_ci_widths already answers, WITH A CITED RULING,
// that a host holds NO CI slots at all (the arm at committed_width_of: RunnerWidthDerived { slots: 0 }). Such
// a host cannot hand over a slot it does not hold, and the plan says so in its own units:
// gunbc.runner.runner_capacity_plan::committed_width_after_reservation refuses a host whose held slots exceed
// its committed population (ReservationExceedsCommittedWidth) -- which is exactly the refusal the regroup
// plan row serving-build-pool-for-zero-ci-host names as its RED CONTROL: "a reservation refused for want of a
// carved runner slot on a host that has no runners by ruling". The refusal is about the wrong fact. On such a
// host the pool's memory never lived in CI width at all: it is memory a ruling released from elsewhere.
//
// THE RULE: A ZERO-CI HOST'S BUILD POOL IS RULED, NOT CARVED. A host with a ruled row holds exactly that
// reservation, citing the ruling that rules it -- the same shape as ruled_zero_ci_widths, which settled the
// mirror question ("WHY A HOST TAKES NO CI SLOTS IS A RULING, NOT AN ABSENCE"). srv14 (butch) is the first
// and only row: the operator's ruling of 2026-11-24 (verbatim: "yes you can retire the 192gib serving
// reservation") released the retired 2 x 96 GiB Strata-engine charge TO BUTCH'S REMOTE-BUILD POOL, NOT TO CI
// -- the interpretation recorded on the ruling itself -- so the pool's appropriation is that ruled 192 GiB,
// CI's committed width stays the ruled zero, and NOTHING is held back from CI width: the width plan converts
// only the carved portion (host_build_pool_reservation_from_ci_width below), so the ruled host's held-slot
// count is zero over a committed population of zero -- CeilingCleared, never ReservationExceedsCommittedWidth.
//
// INVARIANTS, PINNED BY test.claim.runner.runner_capacity_plan_witness (they cannot be checked IN this module
// without importing gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation, which imports THIS module -- the check lives where
// both sides are visible): (a) a host with a ruled build-pool row is also in ruled_zero_ci_widths -- a ruled
// pool on a host still spending CI width would double-promise the same bytes; (b) it carries no ledger of its
// own -- such a host's reservation reaches the envelope through
// gunbc.serving.serving_reservation::host_memory_reserved, and charging it again here would re-open the
// double-count the host-keyed partition fixed; (c) a ruled row REPLACES the carve for its host (the ruled arm
// is tried first), so a phantom slot never coexists with the ruled amount. A ruled row is a RULING'S
// PROJECTION, not a second opinion: it carries the ruling's DeclarationRef the way ruled_zero_ci_widths
// carries its ruling's, and the roster still gates when either form fires -- a pool is never held for a host
// the dispatch roster has not admitted (gunbc.design_argument::consumption).
//
// THE QUANTITY IS NOT THE ENVELOPE AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE: this is what the pool may PROMISE (the ledger
// appropriation, the user-work slice ceiling, the seats it divides), taken from the ruling's own number. What
// the machine actually has stays the envelope's fact (gunbc.host.host_memory_observation), and a request is
// still adjudicated against live MemAvailable (gunbc.compute.host_capacity's backing relation). A ruling that
// released more than the host can hold would be caught there, not here.
type RuledBuildPoolReservation {
  host: HostIdentity
  ruling: DeclarationRef
  reservation: ByteSize
}

data ruled_build_pool_reservations: List<RuledBuildPoolReservation> = [
  RuledBuildPoolReservation {
    host: operator_host_srv14,
    ruling: srv14_serving_memory_charge_retirement_operator_approved_2026_11_24.reference,
    // 192 GiB: the released charge counted in the operator's own number ("the 192gib serving reservation") --
    // 2 x 96 GiB of strata-engine memory_max, the rows gunbc#17005 retired together with the srv14
    // ServingHostReservation. The quiescent read behind the release is
    // gunbc.host.host_memory_observation::srv14_memavailable_quiescent_read (fleet-converge run 45321352046).
    reservation: byte_size(206158430208),
  },
]

// THE ONE LOOKUP: the ruled amount for a host, if a ruling rules one. Absent is NOT zero -- it says this
// host's pool follows the carve, a different fact (fail_closed: absence and value do not collapse).
fn host_ruled_build_pool_reservation(host: HostIdentity) -> ByteSize? {
  match first_where(ruled_build_pool_reservations, r => (r.host as String) == (host as String)) {
    Present { value: r } => Present { value: r.reservation }
    Absent => Absent
  }
}

// THE PREDICATE FORM, for readers (witnesses, the plan's equivalence) that must ask "is this host's pool
// ruled" without re-deriving it from the amount -- the host_build_pool_is_carved pattern.
fn host_build_pool_is_ruled(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  ruled_build_pool_reservations.any(r => (r.host as String) == (host as String))
}

// THE ONE COMPOSITION, PURE over the two host facts, so the positive case is witnessed BEFORE the roster
// admits the host (the build_pool_carve_is_due pattern, review 95809): a ruled amount answers; otherwise a
// due carve answers with exactly one CI runner slot; otherwise nothing.
fn build_pool_reservation_of_ruling(ruled: ByteSize?, carve_due: Bool) -> ByteSize {
  match ruled {
    Present { value: amount } => amount
    Absent => if carve_due { gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max } else { byte_size(0) }
  }
}

// WHAT CI ACTUALLY GAVE UP FOR THIS HOST'S POOL -- the quantity the WIDTH PLAN converts into held-back slots
// (gunbc.runner.runner_capacity_plan remote_build_pool_slot_reservation). A ruled pool was never CI's to give,
// so its from-CI amount is zero; a carved pool is CI's, in full. Everything that holds memory -- the ledger
// appropriation and seat (gunbc.compute.host_capacity host_capacity_build_seat), the user-work slice ceiling,
// and the envelope's build-pool charge at gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation envelope_final -- keeps reading
// host_build_pool_reservation: the pool holds the ruled bytes ONCE, and conservation charges them there. This
// function separates only the question the plan asks, "how many CI slots is this host not spending", and on a
// host whose CI width a ruling already took to zero the honest answer is zero.
fn host_build_pool_reservation_from_ci_width(host: HostIdentity) -> ByteSize {
  match host_ruled_build_pool_reservation(host: host) {
    Present { value: _ } => byte_size(0)
    Absent => host_build_pool_reservation(host: host)
  }
}

// THE SLOT CARRIES NO ABSOLUTE CPU CEILING, BY THE SAME RULING THAT SETS cores_per_slot TO ONE.
// This used to be CpuQuotaResolved at the derived cores_per_slot, which with the policy at 1 would
// render CPUQuota=100% on every runner unit and fabric cell slice -- a ceiling that throttles the
// parallel link and codegen phases of the very single-core-sustained workload the ruling describes.
// The hosts run with CPUQuota unlimited today; this arm says so rather than emitting a new
// constraint the ruling did not ask for. cpu_weight stays the relative-share governor.
//
// GITHUB RUNNER SLOTS NO LONGER READ THIS ROW (operator ruling 2026-10-14, "slots need a CPU cap").
// Their ceiling is per host, because it divides that host's threads among that host's width:
// gunbc.ci.ci_runner_placement host_cpu_allocation. This fleet-wide arm still governs the fabric
// cell, microvm and workspace slices that consume it; whether those take a per-host allocation too
// is their own change, and until it lands the two are deliberately different answers for different
// slot families, not one fact stated twice.
fn gunbc_runner_slot_cpu_quota() -> RunnerSlotCpuQuota {
  SlotCpuQuotaUnbounded
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_desired() -> RunnerSlotDesired {
  RunnerSlotDesired {
    memory_max: gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max,
    memory_swap_max: byte_size(0),
    memory_high: gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max,
    tasks_max: 16384,
    cpu_weight: 100,
    cpu_quota: gunbc_runner_slot_cpu_quota(),
    labels: RunnerSlotLabels { github_labels: ["gunbc-heavy"] }
  }
}

// WHO OWNS A BOUNDED CGROUP INSIDE A SLOT -- a fleet fact, kept beside the envelope it governs and
// consumed by gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate native_step_memory_inputs to decide whether the
// required subject steps ask gunbc test for a per-step bind. It is a fact about the hosts, not a
// rung drop: nothing on the required path ever held the per-step trip (main's witnesses.yml never
// carried the bind input), so the climb it blocks is rostered as a stall --
// gunbc.guarantee_stall native_step_trip_awaits_fleet_job_cgroup_stall -- and this row flips when
// the fleet earns the capability that stall names, observed on the hosts and nowhere else.
type SlotJobCgroupOwnership
  = JobOwnsNoBoundedCgroup { why: String }
  | JobOwnsBoundedCgroup { staged_by: String }

data gunbc_runner_slot_job_cgroup_ownership: SlotJobCgroupOwnership = JobOwnsNoBoundedCgroup {
  why: "a fleet job runs as the setpriv'd job user under a root unit (gunbc.runner.runner_host_deploy unit_user and job_user) and memory_governor apply_memory_cgroup_bind creates its leaf at the cgroup root, so no process of a job can create or join a bounded cgroup; the first required run that asked refused in four seconds (run 38002497388, MemoryCgroupBindRefused)"
}
